vtkOpenGLRenderer: UpdateLightingUniformsEveryRender, for renderers that share shader programs

The renderers of a render window share its shader programs (one
vtkOpenGLShaderCache per window). vtkOpenGLRenderer::UpdateLightingUniforms()
skips a program when the program's LightingGroup update time is not older
than the renderer's lights and active camera. That time is kept on the
program, not per renderer, so it does not say whose lights the program holds.

When two renderers of one window draw props that use the same program (the
same kind of mapper, with the same number and kind of lights), the renderer
whose lights and camera were modified first finds the program up to date
after the other renderer has set its light uniforms. It then draws with the
other renderer's light colors, and with light directions and positions in the
other renderer's view coordinates. Which renderer draws wrong depends only on
the order of the modification times, so it changes with the order in which an
application creates or modifies cameras and lights. A main view and an inset
view of the same props in one window show it: in libcvc's viewports the main
view's surfaces were lit with the inset's lights, and which props were lit
wrong differed between Linux and Windows.

This adds vtkOpenGLRenderer::UpdateLightingUniformsEveryRender. Off, the
default, nothing changes. On, UpdateLights() counts the renderer's lights as
modified at the start of every render (a new time stamp), so the renderer
sets its light uniforms in each program the first time it draws with that
program in a render, and skips the program for the rest of the render as
before. The cost is one light-uniform update per program per render.

It is opt-in so that the default draw path stays as it is. Making it the
default, or keying the cache by renderer as well as by program, would fix
every application, at the cost of that update. A renderer that leaves it off
can still draw with the light uniforms of one that has it on, so an
application turns it on for every renderer of the window.

TestLightingUniformsEveryRender draws a sphere in each of two renderers of
one window, lit from the left in one and from the right in the other, and
checks that each renderer draws as it does alone. With the property off, the
left sphere is lit from the right from the first render with both renderers
on.

